      lecture: [
        { say: 'Welcome to modelling. This lesson is about the simplest model there is: a quantity that changes at a rate proportional to its own size.', show: { type: 'board', title: 'Proportional change', tex: String.raw`y' = ky` } },
        { say: 'A drug in the bloodstream, a radioactive sample, money with continuous interest, a bacteria culture: they all follow this one equation.' },
        { say: 'You solved it in the last module. With y of 0 equal to y nought, the solution is y equals y nought times e to the k t.', show: { type: 'board', tex: String.raw`y' = ky,\quad y(0) = y_0 \;\Longrightarrow\; y = y_0\,e^{kt}` } },
        { say: 'If k is positive, y grows. If k is negative, y decays. The unit of k is one over the unit of time, so that k times t has no unit.', show: { type: 'board', tex: String.raw`k > 0:\ \text{growth} \qquad k < 0:\ \text{decay}` } },
        { say: 'Doubling time and half-life come straight from the formula: the natural log of 2, divided by the size of k.', show: { type: 'board', tex: String.raw`t_{\text{double}} = \frac{\ln 2}{k} \qquad t_{\text{half}} = \frac{\ln 2}{|k|}` } },
        { say: 'Every model in this module follows the same four moves. Name the unknown and its units, translate the words into an equation, solve it, and answer the question that was asked.', clear: true, show: { type: 'board', title: 'Four moves', html: '1. name the unknown and units<br>2. words into the ODE<br>3. solve<br>4. answer the question, with units' } },
        { say: 'Answering the question usually means solving e to the a t equals b for t. Take the natural log of both sides: a t equals log b, so t equals log b over a.', show: { type: 'board', title: 'Refresher', tex: String.raw`e^{at} = b \;\Longrightarrow\; at = \ln b \;\Longrightarrow\; t = \frac{\ln b}{a}` } },
        { say: 'And one fact we will use again and again: the log of one over b is minus the log of b.', show: { type: 'board', tex: String.raw`\ln\tfrac1b = -\ln b` } },
        { say: 'Here is the example. A patient has 50 milligrams of a drug in the body, and it is eliminated at the rate 0.2 times A milligrams per hour, where A is the amount present.', clear: true, show: 3 },
        { say: 'Move 1: A of t is the amount in milligrams, t is the time in hours. Move 2: elimination makes A smaller, so there is a minus sign. A prime equals minus 0.2 A, and A of 0 is 50.' },
        { say: 'Move 3: solve. First separate: divide both sides by A, so that every A is on the left.', show: 4, step: 0, moves: { 'A-1': 'The A on the right crosses the equals sign and lands under the 1 on the left: 1 over A, times d A by d t.' } },
        { say: 'Multiply both sides by d t. Now only A is on the left, and only t on the right.', step: 1, moves: { 'dt-1': 'The d t leaves the bottom of the left side and arrives on the right, next to minus 0.2.' } },
        { say: 'Integrate both sides. The integral of d A over A is the log of the absolute value of A. The right side gives minus 0.2 t, plus one constant.', step: 2 },
        { say: 'Take e to the power of both sides. e to the C 1, with its plus or minus, is just a constant, which we call C. So A equals C e to the minus 0.2 t.', step: 3 },
        { say: 'Now the initial condition. At t equals 0, e to the 0 is 1, so A of 0 equals C, and C is 50.', step: 4, moves: { 'C-2': 'The 50 from the initial condition slides into the place of C. A of t equals 50 e to the minus 0.2 t milligrams.' } },
        { say: 'Move 4: the question as an equation. When are 10 milligrams left? Set A of t equal to 10.', step: 5 },
        { say: 'Divide both sides by 50, to free the exponential.', step: 6, moves: { 'f-1': 'The 50 leaves the left side and lands under the 10 on the right: 10 over 50, which is one fifth.' } },
        { say: 'Take the natural log of both sides. The log of e to the minus 0.2 t is minus 0.2 t, and the log of one fifth is minus log 5.', step: 7 },
        { say: 'Divide both sides by minus 0.2. The two minus signs cancel, and dividing by 0.2 is multiplying by 5. t equals 5 log 5, about 8.05 hours.', step: 8 },
        { say: 'Notice the units at the end. A time without units is only half an answer.' },
        { say: 'Your turn. A sample of 80 grams decays with y prime equals minus 0.5 y, with t in days. When are 20 grams left? Same moves, one step at a time.', clear: true, show: 5 },
        { say: 'Well done. Same pattern: C is the start value, divide by it, take logs, divide by minus k. 2 log 4 is about 2.77 days.' },
        { say: 'A quick check about half-lives.', show: 6 },
        { say: 'Right: 24 days are three half-lives, so one half, times one half, times one half, which is one eighth, remains.' },
        { say: 'To sum up: proportional change means y prime equals k y, and the solution is y nought e to the k t.', clear: true, show: { type: 'board', title: 'Summary', tex: String.raw`y' = ky \;\Longrightarrow\; y = y_0\,e^{kt}` } },
        { say: 'To find a time: free the exponential, take logs, divide. Mark the lesson complete when you are ready.' },
      ],
